  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nA weak amplitude shortwave has been propagating through today, with \nscattered showers associated with isentropic ascent moving \ndownstream of the CWA this afternoon. In the wake of this precip, \nskies have been varying between overcast in the southern half of the \nCWA to mostly sunny near the International Border. Conditions stay \non the quieter side this afternoon into tonight as a 500mb ridge \nbriefly slides over the area. This ridge will be fairly messy though \nwith multiple vorticity maxima propagating through tonight into \nearly tomorrow morning, which could develop an isolated shower. \nDespite these shots of vorticity coming through, keeping the \npotential low at a 20-25% chance due to a significant cap expected \nto develop around 800mb as southerly winds bring WAA to the area. \n\nThis WAA advection will usher in warmer temperatures on Sunday for \nthe southern edge of the CWA, with highs increasing to the low 80s \nand dewpoint temps becoming sticky in the mid to upper 60 degree \nrange. The aforementioned capping inversion will likely inhibit \nconvective initiation through the daytime hours on Sunday with skies \nvarying between partly to mostly cloudy.\n\nA warm front associated with vertically-stacked low pressure over \nthe Canadian Prairies is anticipated to lift north on Sunday night, \nbringing scattered showers and thunderstorms that will continue \nuntil Monday morning. A sizable amount of instability will be \nassociated with the warm front, with MUCAPE from CAMs indicating\npotential values in the 1500-2000 J/kg range on Sunday night \naround the Brained Lakes towards the I-35 corridor. This will \nalso be accompanied by favorable 0-6km bulk shear up to 40 \nknots. However, it's worth noting that this is a large \ndivergence from global models, which put timing of peak \ninstability and shear later into Monday. This increases \nuncertainty with this warm fropa. If timing of peak instability \nhappens more overnight, large hail to ping pong ball size is the\nprimary hazard with damaging winds being unlikely due to the \nlow-level cap. SPC has the southwestern corner of the CWA in a \nMarginal Risk on Sunday night. \n\nOn Monday, the severe thunderstorm potential shifts into northwest \nWisconsin as the occluded front associated with the sfc low moves \nthrough. Again, impacts on Monday will largely be dependent on \ntiming of this occluded fropa. If the earlier timing becomes \nfavored, most of the convection will likely be exiting by early \nMonday afternoon and severe storms will be limited to elevated \nhailers. \n\nIf the later timing favored in global models, such as the GFS, \noccurs then this front will be sweeping through northwest Wisconsin \nduring peak diurnal heating and allow for 1500-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE \nto develop. With 0-6km bulk shear remaining around 40-45 knots,\nthis could lead to supercell development. If supercells \ndevelop, all hazards are possible including damaging winds to 70\nmph, large hail up to golf ball size, and locally heavy rain \nwith PWAT to 1.6\". A tornado or two also can't be ruled out as \nhodographs indicate a moderately favorable 60-100 m2/s2 of SRH \navailable to right-moving supercells. This is highly uncertain \nat this time though given the discrepancies in timing between \nthe various models. \n\nFollowing the occluded frontal passage on Monday, a more benign \npattern settles over the region for mid-week with slightly cooler \nhigh temps. Low pressure, following a typical Colorado Low type \ntrack, will bring a chance for additional showers and thunderstorms \non Wednesday. Additional precipitation will be possible later in the \nweek as the 500mb pattern remains active.",
